// Kernel-methods via `@derive` — impl-site diagnostics, IDENTICAL to the `impl`-form ones because
// `@derive(vec.Kernels)` funnels into the same bundle-binding validation. A constraint mismatch
// (a `bool` vector can't satisfy `vec.Kernels`; a FLOAT vector can't satisfy the integer-only
// `vec.SatKernels`) is the same E0015 an empty `impl` would give. Binding the SAME bundle twice —
// once by `@derive`, once by `impl` — is a coherence duplicate (E0027), exactly as two `impl`
// blocks or two derives of a trait would be. A dotted name in a known module that names no bundle
// is an unknown trait (E0014).
// expect: error E0015 at 17:9
// expect: error E0015 at 22:9
// expect: error E0027 at 29:6
// expect: error E0014 at 33:9
// expect: exit 1
use std.{vec}

// Ineligible shape — `vec.Kernels` needs ≥2 numeric fields; a bool vector has none (E0015).
@packed
@derive(vec.Kernels)
struct Flags { a: bool; b: bool }

// `vec.SatKernels` is integer-only (saturation is undefined for floats) — a float vector is E0015.
@packed
@derive(vec.SatKernels)
struct FVec3 { x: f32; y: f32; z: f32 }

// The SAME bundle bound twice — `@derive` then `impl` — is one binding too many (E0027).
@packed
@derive(vec.Kernels)
struct V3 { x: f32; y: f32; z: f32 }
impl vec.Kernels for V3 {}

// A dotted name in a bound module that resolves to no bundle is an unknown trait (E0014).
@packed
@derive(vec.Nope)
struct W3 { x: f32; y: f32; z: f32 }
